# No subject or civilian names on the public site

## Redact (never name in `*.html`, speeches, news cards, sources labels, or new catalog narrative)

- **Subjects** of alleged, charged, indicted, convicted, fired, resigned-for-misuse, or under-investigation ALPR / Flock cases (officers, deputies, civilian employees, dispatchers, chiefs **when they are the accused**)
- **Civilians** those cases involve: targets, partners, “exes,” family members, victims, and their relatives

Prefer: “one officer,” “a deputy,” “an unnamed employee,” “a civilian,” “someone known to the officer.”

## Required sentence (accused / charged / fired / investigated)

On public pages, every new or updated mention of a misuse subject uses this shape:

**[Source] reported that [a job] from [agency or city] was [event] with [the official accusation or employment action].**

Example: “GBI reported that a deputy from the Clayton County Sheriff’s Office was charged with misuse of a license-plate reader system.”

| Slot | Fill with | Never |
|---|---|---|
| Source | Linked outlet or official paper (GBI, PD, docket) | This site as the finder of guilt |
| Job | officer, deputy, civilian employee, former officer | A personal name |
| Agency or city | Clayton County Sheriff’s Office, Hahira PD | Home address, precinct gossip |
| Event | charged, arrested, fired, resigned, recommended for firing, under investigation | “misused Flock,” “stalked,” “is guilty” in our voice |
| Accusation / action | the charge or HR action **as the source stated it** | Civilian names, “ex,” plates, kids, extra counts we cannot source |

Same formula for arrests and firings (“WALB reported that an officer from Hahira PD was fired, then arrested…”). Do not put the accused’s name in headlines, body, sources labels, JSON narrative, or lookup Outcome.

## Keep

- **Official sources** speaking or acting on the record who are **not** the accused: sheriff, chief, mayor, city manager, councilmember announcing a decision or quoted (“Sheriff X said…”)
- Cities, agencies, Case_IDs / wrongful IDs, outlet names
- Vendor / CEO pages when the story is the company, not a misuse subject

## Edge case

If the announcing official **is** the accused (e.g. a chief charged or fired for personal lookups), **error on caution: do not name them.** Treat them as a subject.

## Catalog JSON

`Subject_Officer_Involved` may keep **role words** for the misuse lookup Outcome column (`officer`, `deputy`, `civilian employee`). Do **not** put personal names there. Do not put civilian names in any skinny index field.

## Anchors

Prefer generic slugs (`#oak-ridge-misuse`, `#lufkin-misuse`). Keep old name-based IDs only as hidden aliases so existing links do not break.

